    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Credential Access — Valid Accounts: Default Accounts", "technique": "T1078.001", "what_happened": "At 2026-02-11 09:14:03 CET, sshd on host 10.108.57.184 logged a burst of authentication failures originating from data.srcip=45.33.32.156. The credential pairs attempted were vendor-default pairs: admin:admin, root:root, admin:password, admin:changeme. Each attempt arrived ~8 seconds apart over a ~8-minute window. Wazuh rule 5710 ('Multiple failed logins from single IP') fired once the per-source failure counter crossed its threshold of 5. The attempted usernames (admin, root) are well-known default management accounts; the passwords are the most common vendor-shipped defaults. This is automated default-credential scanning, not a targeted brute-force of a known account.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 5710 triggered with data.srcip=45.33.32.156 against a single data.dstip=10.108.57.184 on dstport 22, with rule.mitre.id including T1078.001. The failure events share one source IP and one destination, with targetUserName cycling through default account names.", "first_seen": "2026-02-11T09:14:03+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access — Valid Accounts: Default Accounts", "technique": "T1078.001", "what_happened": "At 09:22:40 CET (immediately after the SSH phase), the same data.srcip=45.33.32.156 switched protocol and issued HTTP POST requests carrying HTTP Basic Authorization headers with default credential pairs (admin:admin, test:test, guest:guest, tomcat:tomcat, manager:manager) against management endpoints on the web service of 10.108.57.184 — /admin/login, /manager/html, /administrator, /login, /webadmin. The web server returned 401/403 for every pair. Wazuh rule 5711 ('Default credential attempt detected') fired on the recognised default-credential + management-endpoint pattern. The User-Agent string 'Mozilla/5.0 Chrome/120.0.0.0' was static across all requests — consistent with an automated credential scanner, not a human browser session.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 5711 triggered with data.srcip=45.33.32.156 against data.dstip=10.108.57.184 on dstport 80, rule.mitre.id including T1078.001. Same source IP as step 1, protocol pivot SSH→HTTP, default credential pairs in the Authorization header against well-known management paths.", "first_seen": "2026-02-11T09:22:40+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Credential Access / Discovery — RDP attack-surface probe", "technique": "T1078.001", "what_happened": "At 09:30:05 CET, the same data.srcip=45.33.32.156 opened a TCP connection to port 3389 (RDP) on 10.108.57.184 to confirm whether the Remote Desktop service was exposed. Suricata logged the connection attempt and a single Windows Security 4625 failed-logon event was recorded for targetUserName=administrator from ipAddress=45.33.32.156. No successful 4624 logon followed. This is the attacker confirming the third management protocol on the same host after exhausting SSH and HTTP default-credential lists — a methodical multi-protocol attack-surface sweep against one management host.", "chain_reason": "Suricata connection event plus Windows Security eventID 4625 with data.win.eventdata.ipAddress=45.33.32.156 and data.win.eventdata.targetUserName=administrator on data.dstport 3389, same data.srcip as steps 1 and 2, all against data.dstip=10.108.57.184. rule.mitre.id T1078.001.", "first_seen": "2026-02-11T09:30:05+0100"}
